<!--Post: 2299--><!--Time: 1103505493-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2299"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Secret_squirrel<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Secret_squirrel<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, December 19, 2004 - 5:18 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I was looking at the picture of the Wegerle home and was wondering if anyone knew first, the time of death and how long before she was discovered? <BR> <BR>I ask this because a few things come to mind when I look at this picture.  The photo shows the house in the background and medics transporting the victim to an ambulance.   <BR> <BR>This is only speculation and derived from my protocols and experience as an emergency care provider. <BR> <BR>First she has been backboarded.  This normally isnt done unless there is supected head,neck,or back trauma.  Secondly headblocks are used to stabilize the head.  Once again this is not normally a common practice unless there is the injuries presented above. <BR> <BR>Also from the photograph it is clear she has been intubated and CPR is in progress.  This is not usually performed unless this person is savable and has a chance for survival.  However, she is a young individual with unatural causes of death and if found early enough definitely could be resusitated.   <BR> <BR>It is clear from the photograph that this is someone who must have been found early and had a good chance for survival.  Its apparent that life saving measures are being done. I have never heard of anyone trying to revive a two day old corpse.  Does anyone know the details of this?  Could it be that she wasnt actually dead as BTK may have thought.  Maybe a weak pulse that could not have been detected by palpation?  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2299-->
<!--Post: 2315--><!--Time: 1103530564-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2315"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 20, 2004 - 12:16 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Vicky Wegerle&#39;s assault that led to her death has been pinpointed to between 10:15 am and 10:30 am. <BR>She was seen by a friend taking her school age kids to school at 9 am.  <BR>A nieghbor said he heard an unusual ammount of dog barking activity at around 10:15 am. Her car was seen pulling out of the driveway at 10:30 am. <BR>Her husband returned home from work at 12 noon and discovered her and called paramedics. They took no chances in pronouncing her dead and rushed to the hospital. Her two year old child was in the home and not harmed. <BR>Her car was found two blocks away at 12:10 pm.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2315-->
<!--Post: 2321--><!--Time: 1103540885-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2321"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Secret_squirrel<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Secret_squirrel<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 20, 2004 - 3:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I found the times after I posted this and it&#39;s understandable why life saving measures were underway.  I didnt realize that there was such a small window of time between her death and discovery.  It&#39;s hard to believe that such a dramatic event can happen so quickly.  Thanks again!!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2321-->
<!--Post: 2329--><!--Time: 1103548052-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST2329"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, December 20, 2004 - 5:07 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That&#39;s about 1.5 hours, right?  That&#39;s quite awhile and different from the report somewhere else that her husband could have just missed passing BTK on the street as he left. <BR> <BR>When I was first reading about the case i had the impression he was spending wuite a bit longer at each location<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 2329-->
<!--Post: 2330--><!--Time: 1103548110-->
